  {
    "episode_id": 83280,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83280.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, I think that our framers really wanted people from different walks of life, with different life experiences to get involved. And I may have a blindness in one area, somebody may not, as an intern. And so the very best of the best ideas come to the service. In my case, dentistry has a very different history than medicine. And it comes back to a division in 1964. This is when Medicare started. And so dentistry was at the table and they decided to walk away. And the results have been is that dentistry has got its own problems but is a market-based process. So the same dollar you spent in 1970 is basically the same dollar you spend today with inflation only. Medicine is more than 20 times that plus inflation. The marketplace is artificial. It's not based on what the market absorbs. It's what the government will pay. So it's got a contradictory influence to it. And that's why we find the problems. We can see cost shifting from one side to the left and eventually you run out of time.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The framers intended for individuals from diverse backgrounds and life experiences to participate in governance.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Dentistry has a distinct historical development compared to medicine.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "In 1964, dental services were excluded from Medicare coverage when it was established.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Since 1964, dentistry has operated under a market-based system without significant public funding.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Medicine has seen over 20-fold growth in spending per capita since 1970, adjusted for inflation.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Government reimbursement rates shape healthcare costs in medicine more than actual market demand.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The current medical financing model creates artificial incentives and distortions in pricing.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Cost-shifting occurs within the healthcare system due to misaligned financial incentives.",
        "confidence": 0.84
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
